{
  "teamId": 145,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Chicago White Sox arrived at the season with cautious optimism, and that caution proved warranted almost immediately. Opening weekend brought a gut-punch loss when Christian Yelich tagged Seranthony Domínguez for a three-run homer that swung the game decisively, sending Chicago to a rough 1-4 March. But the South Siders refused to let that early wound fester. April saw the team scrap back to .500 on the strength of clutch, gritty moments — Derek Hill's perfectly-placed bunt single igniting a rally, Tristan Peters driving home the go-ahead run, and Andrew Benintendi announcing his presence with a three-run shot. It wasn't always pretty, but Chicago's character was beginning to reveal itself.\n\nMay became the turning point that changed the White Sox's entire identity. The offense erupted with power and depth that few had anticipated. Edgar Quero's first career homer, a two-run blast that swung a massive 0.576 WPA, announced the arrival of a genuine young force behind the plate. Munetaka Murakami continued to punish right-center gaps with his distinctive swing, and then Miguel Vargas delivered the single most impactful hit of the entire regular season — a walk-off 13th homer worth 0.852 WPA that seemed to crystallize everything this team could be. Chicago went 18-10 in May, their best month, and suddenly 84 wins felt like a real possibility rather than a pipe dream.\n\nJune through August was a grind of survival, momentum swings, and individual heroics. Sam Antonacci's clutch single that survived a Guardians challenge, Braden Montgomery's first career homer in a high-leverage spot, and Randal Grichuk stepping up repeatedly as a veteran stabilizer kept the White Sox above water through the dog days. The pitching staff showed its limitations — Grant Taylor surrendered critical homers, Jordan Hicks and Trevor Richards faltered in tight spots — but the offense, anchored by Vargas's 20th homer in early July and powered by 211 round-trippers on the year, kept the ship from sinking. Chicago finished 84-78, good enough for a postseason berth and a date with the Cleveland Guardians in the ALDS.\n\nThe Division Series told a story of a team that had punched its ticket to October but found a tougher opponent waiting. Murakami's two-run shot in Game 1 gave White Sox fans hope, and Braden Montgomery's sharp double in Game 2 had Chicago feeling dangerous. But Jo Adell proved to be the series villain, tripling twice to swing momentum Cleveland's way, and José Ramírez — the Guardians' October tormentor — homered in back-to-back games off Hicks and Sean Burke to close the door. A devastating double play in Game 3 snuffed out a critical Chicago rally, and the White Sox were eliminated, their promising 84-win season ending one round short of the American League Championship Series. The foundation, though, looks real.",
